# Controller Patterns

Controllers stay thin: strong params, auth, call a process, render. No business logic or SQL.

Use compact class names (`class API::V1::User::RegistrationsController`) and a leading `::` when calling processes so `User` never collides with `API::V1::User`.

```ruby
class API::V1::User::RegistrationsController < API::V1::BaseController
  skip_before_action :authenticate_user!

  def create
    case ::User::Registration.call(permitted_params)
    in Solid::Success(user:)
      render_json_with_success(status: :created, message: I18n.t("user.registration.success"), data: { user: ::User::Serializer.new(user) })
    in Solid::Failure(input:)
      render_json_with_model_errors(input)
    else
      render_json_with_error(status: :unprocessable_content, message: I18n.t("user.registration.failure"))
    end
  end

  private

  def permitted_params
    params.permit(:first_name, :last_name, :email, :password, :password_confirmation)
  end
end
```

- Public endpoints: `skip_before_action :authenticate_user!`.
- Protected endpoints: inherit `authenticate_user!` and pass `user: current_user`.
- Session create: merge `request_metadata` (`ip_address`, `user_agent`) into params.
- Refresh: read `cookies.encrypted[:refresh_token]`; on success call `set_refresh_token_as_cookie`.
- Match `Solid::Failure(input:)` regardless of failure type; `else` is the unexpected fallback.
- Render via `render_json_with_success` / `render_json_with_error` / `render_json_with_model_errors` only.

Reference: `API::V1::User::RegistrationsController` (public), `API::V1::User::ProfilesController` (authenticated).
